From: sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk (Stephen J. Whitrow)
Date: Wed, 09 Mar 94 01:20:43 GMT
Subject: [#94-3-85] LANG: Spontaneous orders (Was: ANTHRO: Multiple origins of modern homo-sapien)

JUMP IN THE FIRE says:
>Most attempts to come up with an artificial 'universal language' fail
>because their aim is grammatical simplicity rather than clearer and
>more descriptive communication in less time.  Why bother to learn a
>new language that doesn't do any more for you than the old one did?

Most attempts to replace spontaneous orders with artificial substitutes
fail, since the substitutes, by definition, are either inefficient
imposed orders or a minority interest.  <I like the suggested usage of
brokets, but applied to sentences though, since it allows me to interpose
comments such as this.>  <I'm not trying to say your explanation in this
specific case is wrong, just exploring what general rules exist, whether
this case satisfies a general rule, etc....>

There are two levels of spontaneous order here: firstly, each of the
natural languages; secondly, the free market for languages that they exist
in.  <There are really many more levels, e.g. below the language free
market we have brain function, and below this comes regulation of the
Earth's ecosystem.  {My notation would have a level of s.o. which was a
necessary precursor to another as 'below' the latter, since it could be
considered as the foundation upon which others arise.}  But I'll leave
these for now.>

Freely competing artificial languages are likely to be a minority interest
for quite some time yet.  <I'm not sure if your definition of 'failure' is
failure to win acceptance by the majority of the world's population, or
failure to win acceptance by a significant minority.>  Maybe descriptive
communication would win a larger minority share than grammatical
simplicity, and eventually artificial languages could even evolve into the
natural choice of the majority.  This would be an example of a voluntarily
implemented designed order.  <They exist in (or "above") a spontaneous
order.>  However, I'd consider this course of events extremely unlikely
before the migration to silicon.

On the other hand, it would be a tragedy if an artificial universal
language were imposed by an international authority.  Designed orders
which exist in imposed orders tend to be despised and of low quality.
<Look at Russian TV sets, for instance, they're quite useful if you want
to burn the house down.>  Illiteracy would rocket.  ~Aint no sutch fing az
iliterusea iss a fikshun maid up beye le roolin klasiz~  <~I've just
ordered my alter ego to "get off that keyboard now!"  If he does it again
I'll make him stand outside the local newspaper offices until someone
jumps out and lands a right hook on him.                   [editor]~>

Still, spontaneous orders aren't the only winning game in town.  Consider
another example of the voluntarily implemented designed order.  Once we
start re-engineering ourselves --  e.g. speeding our brains up by six
orders of magnitude, or even just eliminating "natural" death -- it'll
make biological genetic evolution as redundant as a man with a flag
walking in front of Concorde.  Any advantage gained through genetic
mutation would pale into insignificance.  And even if self-transformation
is only a minority interest, it won't be a losing strategy.  <And when
I've speeded my brain up by a million times and incorporated infallible
random access indexing, I might want to learn a universal language.>

So spontaneous orders are very useful things, and designed orders can on
occasion be even better, but only in the absence of imposed orders.

