>with this sort of organized crime problem.

Right, the means of extortion.

George O'Brien argues that the 4 pillars of the State are the income
tax, the central bank, the military, and State-schooling.  Now consider
the effects of digital cash on these:

1) Digital cash v. the income tax: since digital cash is more secure
against traces and theft than other forms of currency, it will make
income taxation more difficult.

2) Digital cash v. the central bank: since digital cash can be transerred
from place to place or person to person at the speed of light, again without
a trace, it will provide people with easier access to currencies besides
those of the central bank, thus reducing the influence of the central bank
upon the economy and making it easier for people to protect themselves
against inflation by instantaneous flight either to more stable foreign
currency or blackmarket (a.k.a. "counter-economy") currency backed by gold
or whatever other commodity people prefer.

3) Digital cash v. the military: standing armies, like all functions of the
State, can only be financed 3 ways: taxation, bonds, and inflation (a.k.a.
extortion, extortion futures, and hyperextortion).  Since bonds can only
be paid out of future taxes (extortion futures can only depend upon future
extortion), the fact that digital cash will make taxation less feasible will
also make bonds less feasible.  And, since it will also make inflation less
feasible, it will make it less feasible for the State to finance itself at
all, across the board.  This will effect the military by making it less
able to externalize its costs upon the people, thus forcing it to either
internalize its costs or cut its expenditures - probably both.  I'd expect
a trend towards subscriber-support for military projects than can't be
easily supported by small groups of people, as well as the offloading of
military defense from the standing army to the militia as far as hand-held
weaponry and any other projects than can be supported by small groups of
people, such as intelligence and communications capabilities.

4) Digital cash v. State schooling: the greater infeasibility of State
financing will effect State schooling as well.  Since people will be more
able to direct the expenditure of their money, they will have greater
bargaining power when it comes to schooling, and greater ability to send
students to private schools, where they will be more likely to be taught
how to think for themselves, question authority, and less likely to be
taught to always obey orders without a second thought.

Good trends, I think, and ones that can be supported at a profit.  Tim
May might have dismissed all this as obvious if he were still on the list,
but I find it more useful to try to draw out the concrete implications of
these things than he seemed to.

