> >From: Eric Watt Forste <arkuat@netcom.com> (as quoted by Tim Starr)
> > I called PCR and evolutionary epistemology richer 
> >and broader and more complex because, for starters, they add to the 
> >criticism of logical inconsistency (the only one Socrates was good at 
> >using) the vitally important criticism of empirical fact, and well as 
> >less-well-established criticisms such as Ockham's Razor, lack of 
> >explanatory power, etc etc.

  I'm very interested in criticisms like these, both to learn about
them myself and to encourage other people to use them.  I think it
would be great if people could be trained to apply criticisms to their
own beliefs--there'd be a lot less illogical beliefs in the world.
  For example, one of my pet peeves is various religious-inspired
beliefs that various members of my family have, on questions like
"when is a fetus human."  To these beliefs I apply a criticism I call
"symmetry," which I state as: "If two people use the same method and
the same starting point, and arrive at incompatible conclusions, then
the method is useless and the conclusions should be ignored."  Note
that I don't say the conclusions should be rejected--if someone has a
different reason for arriving at one of them, then that should be paid
attention to.
  Unfortunately, people don't often want to apply criticisms to their
beliefs, and people who do want to use them don't know which ones to
use.  What's a good source for work on identifying valid criticisms?
Has any work been done on teaching the general population to use them?
I think a major problem with teaching people who have some logical
training to use other criticisms is that they think predicate logic is
the only thing they have to worry about--they start at their
assumptions, and as long as they don't break a rule of predicate logic
they think they can believe any idea they can derive.

