> like a high-performance car that is used mostly by Sunday drivers.  In
> skilled hands, it makes us realize that the limitations are in us,
> individually, and not in the medium.
> 
> I got this attitude from reading a lot poetry.

Our language can be intensely powerful, but slow.  Often I feel I must
use a series of several analogies or clauses to adequately convey
my feeling -- and conversation does not leave me time to speak as
clearly or concisely as I desire, since the topic will drift before
I can polish my diction.

Once, in the heat of an long discussion/argument with a friend in
which the desire to be heard often prefigured a coherent organization
of our thoughts, I suggested we allow ourselves silence during which
we could think -- a precept of many Native American cultures when
discussing weighty matters.  The change was remarkable -- we reached
an understanding quickly without being drawn down many of the obvious
blind alleys of argument.

Sadly, my nature is to talk, and talk fast, mostly in self-defense against
our culture where silence is treated as assent rather than dissent.
This is a fundamental difference between white settler culture and
indigenous Amerind cultures, and makes a tremendous difference in the
workings of everyday life.

How?

If silence is assent, then secrecy is a good strategy, since bad 
consequences can be justified by the argument "Well, no one said
anything..."  This assumption underlies the machinations of Big 
Government (tm), Big Business (c), and all other large oppressive
organizations, and gives them a specious but good-sounding excuse.
Of course, there is no way that one person can ever find, keep track
of, or attempt to redress all the freedoms denied hir by
people/organizations now and in the future.

So: to my mind, questioning this assumption is central to building
any sort of value system that will forestall the growth of oppresive
institutions.

Hmm.  Talk about roaring off on a tangent...

