   Aha, but we have one-upped the Amerind by inventing the marvelous
technology of e-mail/news and usenet quoting. Even in IRC/Chat systems,
there is a tendency not to listen to the other person and to try and
talk fast, talk over them, and interupt them when a thought jumps
into one's head.  E-mail solves this problem by allowing the conversation
to be asynchronous and distributed. A 'moment of silence' is inefficient
since it's a synchronous strategy. Some people need more time to think
and research than others, the slowest person holds up the group and
if he doesn't speak up, he loses out onec the conversation proceeds to
new topics.

   Group realtime discussion without e-mail/queuing/buffering leads to
ill thought out conversation as all participants try to get in their points
before the topics are forgotten.

   E-mail discussion satisifies both problems. 1) asynchronous - people
participate at their own rate 2) queuing - discussions are not lost, but
persist. They reach even those people who fall behind the fast talkers.

  In fact, I think e-mail is the ideal platform for education. In Japan,
kids who learn at a faster rate than others are forced to keep pace
with the slowest. This is unfair. In America, kids are are too slow are
forced to keep pace with the average. Also unfair. However, in electronic
learning you proceed at your ideal rate. 

   I think real-time-video-teleconferencing-datahighway-debates are a
mistake. A huge waste of bandwidth which only lowers S/N.

   Of course, before there was e-mail, there were journals and letter
debates, but the transaction cost associated with composing a letter
and mailing it was much higher than today's e-mail. I suspect that
products like Xanadu's 'hypertext' and Telescript technology will make
e-mail even more efficient.

   E-mail, the future of human social communication. (Thaw me out
in 200 years when I have ELM and RN installed in my head. RFC 105761 -
proposal for new neural ELM psychological header, 
